---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const auto = `import { initComponents } from "@skryensya/vanilla/auto";

// Each present selector triggers only its own enhancer's dynamic import.
await initComponents();`;

const scoped = `// A freshly inserted container (a toast, a tab panel)
// does not need to re-scan the whole document.
await initComponents(newNode);`;

const single = `import { mountAccordion } from "@skryensya/vanilla/accordion";

mountAccordion();`;
---

<Base
  title="Automatic mounting"
  description="initComponents() scans the document and import()s only the enhancers the page actually uses."
  documentLayout="grid"
>
  <h1>Automatic mounting</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A page with several system components does not need one import per component:
    <code>initComponents()</code> scans the document, finds which <code>data-sk-*</code> roots are
    present, and <code>import()</code>s only those types.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock code={auto} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>It is a map, not a list of imports</h2>
  <p>
    Under the hood it is a <em>selector → dynamic import</em> table, not a series of static
    <code>import</code> statements. A component the page does not use never crosses the network:
    if there is no <code>[data-sk-select]</code> anywhere in the document, Select's module is
    neither downloaded nor executed. It is the same principle a bundler applies at build time
    (tree-shaking), applied at runtime against the real DOM.
  </p>
  <p>
    The modules that DO match all load in parallel and mount in registry order, so calling
    <code>initComponents()</code> once is enough for an entire page, no matter how many component
    types it combines.
  </p>

  <h2>It is idempotent</h2>
  <p>
    Calling it again does not re-mount what is already mounted: each enhancer marks its own root
    as ready, so <code>initComponents()</code> is safe to invoke more than once on the same page.
  </p>

  <h2>Scope the scan with a root</h2>
  <p>
    By default it scans the whole <code>document</code>, but it accepts any <code>Element</code>.
    When the new content is a known subtree; a toast that was just inserted, a panel that just
    revealed itself; passing it directly skips walking the entire document for one node.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={scoped} label="JavaScript" />

  <h2>What is left out</h2>
  <p>
    <code>CodePreview</code> and <code>ComponentPreview</code> are opt-in documentation surfaces:
    they mount through their own subpath, never as part of an application's default runtime. If
    your page is not a documentation site, you will not need them here.
  </p>

  <h2>The alternative: one single component</h2>
  <p>
    If a page uses exactly one system component, importing its mount function directly skips the
    scan entirely; every component's own page shows this import under "Only &lt;name&gt;". For
    Accordion, for example:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={single} label="JavaScript" />
  <p>
    Reach for this route when you know in advance exactly what you are mounting; reach for
    <code>initComponents()</code> when the page combines several and you would rather not keep
    the list by hand.
  </p>
</Base>
